00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.180
Why do model explanation?

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.360
So when developers look
at model explanation,

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.390
we are typically looking
at model explanation

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:55.960
from the perspective of
understanding the model.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.800
Whereas when the regulators
look at model explanation,

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.930
they are trying to increase
their trust in the model.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.750
These are completely
different goals.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.030
This is why I made
this table in order

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:10.150
to make it clear what
the two goals are.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:12.820
The developers look
at model explanation

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.400
to improve performance
of the models,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.190
whereas regulators look
at model explanation

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:24.040
in order to reduce bias
and increase transparency.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:28.450
Most of our software engineers
would look at model explanation

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:30.610
in order to create
better algorithms,

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.140
in order to create
a better model.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:36.130
Is this a six-layer ResNet
or a 10-layer ResNet?

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.230
Do we do it this
way or that way?

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:40.930
All these differences
are going to come up

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.550
in model explanation.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:46.750
But regulators want to
do model explanation

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.050
to comply to regulations.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.850
And the output for software
engineers and developers

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:57.820
for model explanation is
produce a better model, a better

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:58.960
software.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.470
But regulators want to
produce a better report.

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:06.340
So clearly, the two
audiences are very different.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:10.060
And therefore when you look at
the regulator's perspective,

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:15.140
the entire scope of model
explainability changes.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:17.330
And how you do
the explainability

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:20.430
and how you report
are very different.

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.790
So there's a funny
picture which shows why we

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:24.410
want to do model explanation.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.390
Because we want to
create transparency,

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:28.730
we want to create trust,
we want to create a better

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:30.170
understanding.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.990
And in the US, we
have two guidances.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:38.210
One is the SR 11-7,
and the OC 2011-12,

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:42.440
which are basically guidance
for model risk management.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.440
But when everybody uses
them, then it almost

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.090
becomes mandatory.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:50.080
GDPR in Europe is
very important.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.300
And there are two articles
that are written, one by SCC

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:57.480
and the other by a large number
of academic and industry people

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.840
have written the
article together as

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.480
to how to do the governance
of machine learning.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.990
So types and methods
of machine learning.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:12.190
Considering the time, I'm going
to go through fast on this.

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:16.080
And so model interpretation
has two variants.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.180
One is the global,
the other is local.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.400
Those of you who are
practitioners in machine

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.380
learning know this difference.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:26.190
Global is when we take the
entire aggregate of data

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:29.430
and we want to give the
explanation for the model

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.160
on the entire
collection of data.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.980
Local is for a
given transaction.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:39.120
If we are trying to detect
fraud for one transaction that

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.400
was considered
fraudulent, you want

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:46.890
to know why you identified
that transaction as fraud

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.390
or no fraud.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:52.590
And it can be a single or a
small group of transactions.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:54.720
But local explainability
is basically

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.780
for a limited number of data.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:04.180
So in terms of methods,
we saw that there

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:07.110
are over 30 methods
that are used

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:10.260
in the industry, which
cover a wide scope of model

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:13.200
explainability.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.590
I have only identified
the key ones

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:19.170
that are most commonly
used in the industry

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:23.010
and that are most common
and highly prevalent.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:26.070
So the non-Google ones
that are very common

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:30.270
are LIME, Locally
Interpretable Models.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.640
It's mostly a local model,
I have a brief description.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:36.900
The second is Anchors, which
is by the same authors.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:41.159
And SHAP values, which is
very common in the industry.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:43.860
In terms of the Google
methods, there are two

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:45.040
that I want to highlight.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.780
One is called
integrated gradients.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.890
And that one is very common, and
that has been used very widely

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:53.760
within Google.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.640
I'll describe that in
a little bit of detail.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:00.060
And the second is called
TCAV, Quantitative Testing

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.760
and Concept Activation
Vector, which

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:05.400
is also another
model explainability

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.890
tools that Google has created.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:12.720
So going over these, LIME stands
for Locally Interpretable Model

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:15.000
Agnostic Explanations.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.740
And this is primarily a
local interpretation method,

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:23.300
although there is an extension
to global called SP-LIME.

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:29.400
The key attributes here
is that it generates

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.880
data in the vicinity
of the transaction

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:34.740
or the particular data.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.510
And then it fits
the linear model

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:38.640
and explains the linear model.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.700
It treats the features
as uncorrelated.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.910
And the main issue
with LIME, LIME

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:50.810
is excellent in
explaining local models,

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:53.460
doing a local
interpretation of models.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:56.040
But the problem
here is that it has

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.160
to go through the process
of generating synthetic data

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.920
and fitting the results to that.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.430
So it creates what we call
a surrogate model, which

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:07.560
is problematic in some cases.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:10.470
Some regulators will
not accept that.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.820
And secondly, it
takes time to do that.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:16.020
So if you have a large number
of transactions happening,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:18.240
a million transactions
per second,

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:20.100
it will be very
difficult to apply

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:23.220
this type of a complex
technique on a transaction

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:25.630
by transaction basis.

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:29.110
The second one is an
innovation over LIME,

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:32.250
which is called
Anchors, which is also

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:36.240
a model agnostic
explanation tool.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:44.230
So they basically generate logic
and rules around transactions.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:46.355
And these
high-precision rules are

00:10:46.355 --> 00:10:50.160
called anchors, and present
local sufficient conditions

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:52.050
for these predictions.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:55.380
I have linked the
paper and the GitHub.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:59.520
It's all open source,
it's by the same authors

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:01.710
that wrote the LIME.

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:05.370
This third one that
is publicly available

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:09.360
is called SHAP
Values, it's Shapley.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.230
It basically does the
model by comparing features

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:14.310
with or without it.

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:16.380
If you take the feature
out, it basically

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.290
measures the
sensitivity of a feature

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:22.060
in producing a certain outcome.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:25.590
What is great about
Shapley is that it produces

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.770
the positive and negative
effects on outcomes

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:30.150
for different features.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:33.340
And we have some
results on that.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:37.410
And because you have to take
all combinations of features.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:39.870
If you have, let's say,
a model in which you

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:44.340
have 2,000 features, then taking
different combinations of twos

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:48.780
and threes and four and five
makes millions of combinations.

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:51.780
And therefore it
is very difficult

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.680
to go through all
those combinations.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.970
So they make some approximations
called SABAS methods

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:58.290
and other methods.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.110
And what it does is
that it finally creates

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:02.730
an approximate solution.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:05.670
Nevertheless Shapley
is an awesome method

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:10.470
of doing model explanation and
is widely used in the industry.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:12.840
And it is also very
fast and accurate

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:16.330
in real-time transactions.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:18.820
So coming to the Google
methods, the first one

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.750
is called integrated
gradients, which are axiomatic

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:24.130
attributions of deep networks.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:29.430
Now this one is also a
sensitivity type measure.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:32.410
It is also a local
interpretation tool,

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:36.070
and not geared towards a global
interpretation, although you

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:38.230
can take a whole bunch
of local interpretations,

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:40.870
average them together,
and call it global.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:44.170
So the basic idea is
you perturb the features

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.120
and inspect the
change of the features

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.890
on the network's prediction.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:52.180
This method has been used
in over 20 Google products

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:53.890
internally.

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:58.360
So let's take a visual example
of how this method works.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.630
So here is a picture
of a fireboat

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:03.400
in front of the bridge.

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:06.080
So the model says
there's a fireboat.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:11.560
So if you look at the far
right picture at this end,

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:13.820
you'll see this is the
picture of the fireboat

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:15.790
and these are the gradients.

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:18.850
So the problem with that is that
the gradients doesn't tell you

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:22.420
anything because it covers
the entire gamut of the bridge

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:25.910
and the boat and the
ocean and everything.

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:28.900
So what do we do
in this method is

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:30.850
we take a baseline
picture, which is

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:33.060
a near dark picture beforehand.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.020
And then we look at
the gradients that

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.540
come out of that
picture, and we see

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:40.000
some remnants of the bridge.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:44.320
Then as we change
the picture intensity

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:48.500
from almost dark to
the full intensity,

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:51.070
different gradients emerge.

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:53.680
And as you've progress through
this different intensity

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:56.680
of the picture, and as you
see these different gradients

00:13:56.680 --> 00:14:00.670
as you integrate through
these gradients, then, voila,

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:02.830
the explanation comes out.

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:06.400
And you get a beautiful
picture of the gradients

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.740
that are relevant to
the fireboat itself.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.910
And that's how the integrated
gradient method works.

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:15.730
And we also found
that the gradients

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:19.600
are most relevant during
the transition phase rather

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.210
than the full featured image.

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:29.540
And this is an internal paper
published and available.

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:31.750
So next one is called TCAV.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:36.280
This is an innovation over
the axiomatic methods.

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:39.370
This creates boundaries
of different features

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:42.550
in order to judge the
sensitivity of the feature's

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:45.290
effects on the outputs.

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:50.080
So these are some of the methods
we have for model explanation

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:51.860
that are fairly common.

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:55.060
So next, let's look at
the types of explanations

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:57.820
that regulators
will be looking for.

00:14:57.820 --> 00:15:00.700
The first one that
is most common

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:03.160
is the importance of
the different features.

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:05.600
How are the different
features important?

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:08.890
So if you label the features
one through nine or 10,

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:10.780
then you see that
certain features

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:12.640
have a higher importance.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.560
The second thing you
also want to look

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:17.500
at the standard deviations
of those features.

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:20.060
That is, it's not
important just to be

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:22.030
able to take the
average importance,

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:25.120
but also how does
that importance

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:27.820
vary from sample to sample.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.730
Like if you have an
important such as point,

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.760
let's say, 31, but
the standard deviation

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:38.170
is 20, which means that the
importance of that feature

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.900
varied widely over the samples.

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.240
So you want to also look
at the standard deviations.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:47.060
The second is the positive
and negative contributions

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:48.100
of features.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:52.520
That is, if certain inputs had
a positive effect on the outcome

00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:55.940
and certain inputs had a
negative effect on the outcome,

00:15:55.940 --> 00:16:00.010
so those are the
two important things

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:02.170
that a regulator would want.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:05.650
The other two important
things are joint contributions

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:07.760
and inputs.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:10.270
So let's say you had two inputs.

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:12.550
One was let's say
the loan amount,

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:17.530
and the second one was
the income of the person.

00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:19.390
How does the loan
amount and income

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:25.540
relate to each other in terms
of the risk of credit lending?

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:29.620
So in those two examples,
we'll take some examples

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:30.625
of how that happens.

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:39.020
And also we want to see how does
an input increase the outcome

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:42.380
and how does an input
decrease the outcome.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:47.020
And then fourthly, how
does the different samples

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:51.010
affect the outcome,
the different inputs,

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:54.670
vary from sample to sample
in increasing and decreasing

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:56.510
the outcome.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:59.220
So let us go through
the first example,

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:02.810
and then I'll turn it over to
Adam for the second example.

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:05.089
Loan risk prediction.

00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:09.829
This is a credit to risk
and customer lifecycle value

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:11.930
prediction problem.

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:15.619
The data came from Freddie
Mac's single family home loan.

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:20.030
The ML-based solution is
using user behavior patterns.

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:23.230
And we are predicting
the user risk, which

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:25.859
is the loan delinquency risk.

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:30.800
So this is an example that
is very relevant to cases

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:34.640
like student loan delinquencies,
which has been announced

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:38.010
has gone to 9.1% in some cases.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:41.420
The US is facing student
loan repayment crises.

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:44.660
It can be repurposed to market
loans, student loans, auto

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:47.150
loans, credit card
loans, personal loans.

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.340
The partner on this
project is Pluto7.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:18:01.310
So we basically considered
four classes of outcomes.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:05.220
And one is safe.

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:10.840
We say that the creditor
has been very safe lender,

00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:14.690
or has been very safe
to lend to the person.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:16.850
Second is moderate safe.

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:20.300
And the percentages shows
that how much of the data

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:21.800
was in each of these categories.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:26.400
As you can see, very risky, we
have very, very little data.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.550
So this is a very
unbalanced situation

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:31.820
where we have large amount
of very safe lenders.

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:36.110
And a lot of these
financial services examples,

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:37.740
we have very unbalanced data.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:42.330
That is, one outcome has a
very small amount of data.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:47.590
So we took three sets of data
and we build multiple models

00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:48.620
on that.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:50.930
And the results were very good.

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:54.300
We used deep learning
networks, we used XGBoost,

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:59.250
we used Random Forest, and
we used naive Bayes methods.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:02.720
So by using all these
different algorithms,

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:04.880
we came up with a
very good result

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:10.010
in which the case 1 is safe
and case 2 is moderate safe.

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:14.420
The third one is moderate
risky, and the fourth one

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:16.520
is very risky.

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:20.240
Obviously, you want to catch
the last two very accurately.

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:23.140
And we have 99% accuracy
in the last two,

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:27.710
the class 2 and 3, which
is moderate risk and risky.

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:32.300
And this was a
fantastic result we got.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:38.930
So going to model
explanation on this problem,

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:43.910
I took the top six
inputs that I'm going

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:46.260
to use to explain the models.

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.590
The first one is
recency, which is

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.390
the number of months
ago the customer

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:52.600
had a recorded delinquency.

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:54.620
i stands for number of months.

00:19:54.620 --> 00:19:56.720
Frequency, how
often the customer

00:19:56.720 --> 00:20:01.880
had a recorded loan delinquency
value of which category,

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:03.170
1, 2, 3.

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:06.420
Recency i is the category
delinquency value

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:08.450
with a 0, 1, 2, 3.

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:12.620
0 is most safe, and
3 is most risky.

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:14.510
Credit score.

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:15.800
We all know what that is.

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:17.240
Loan to value.

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:20.420
Loan to value means what
is the value of the asset,

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:23.040
and what is the amount of
loan they took on the asset.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.960
Let's say your home
is worth $200,000,

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:31.500
and you borrowed $100,000,
then your loan to value is 50%.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:35.090
So the original unpaid
balance of the mortgage

00:20:35.090 --> 00:20:38.360
and the original interest
rate on the mortgage.

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:41.990
Again, I can take any one
of these four values-- safe,

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:45.800
moderate safe, mod
risky, and risky.

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:49.670
So then going through
those four examples

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:51.860
that I gave what
the regulators look.

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:53.630
First one is the
future importance.

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:57.620
Second is the positive
and negative contributions

00:20:57.620 --> 00:20:58.800
of inputs.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:01.670
So we found out that
recency score of 2

00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:05.090
was one of the highest
important features, which

00:21:05.090 --> 00:21:09.110
means that if you are
moderately risky before,

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:12.810
you have a high propensity
of becoming risky.

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:17.180
And then recency
score of safe means

00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:20.750
also was a very high
predictor of safety.

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:25.100
And therefore recency score of
0 and 1 were also very high.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:31.070
Unpaid balance is also one
of the high important inputs

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:33.380
in predicting the outcome.

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:36.740
But that doesn't tell
you the whole story.

00:21:36.740 --> 00:21:40.820
I told you that recency of 2 is
probably a predictor of risky,

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:44.360
recency of 0 is probably
a predictor of safe.

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:47.900
So we also want to know
which one of these inputs

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:50.300
had a positive
contribution on the outcome

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:51.620
and a negative contribution.

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:53.412
Which increased
the outcome, which

00:21:53.412 --> 00:21:54.620
decreased the outcome, right?

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:57.740
Which predicted risky,
which predicted safe?

00:21:57.740 --> 00:21:59.820
So that's important to know.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:05.460
So we found that signed
early increased the risk.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:10.340
So the red line shows that if
that particular input increased

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:14.580
the risk, and if any other
input decreased the risk.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:19.550
So you want to know which inputs
had what degree of contribution

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:22.650
towards a positive outcome
or a negative outcome.

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:26.780
And also to what degree,
like signed early

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:31.010
had a huge contribution
in increasing the risk.

00:22:31.010 --> 00:22:32.960
And same as the discount.

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:36.590
Whereas the quantity,
how many loans they took,

00:22:36.590 --> 00:22:40.980
had a very small contribution
in decreasing risk.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:46.250
The second thing is to look at
the correlations of features.

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:49.270
The left chart,
the vertical bars

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:54.990
are 15-year mortgage, 20
year, 25, and 30 year.

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:58.220
And you can see, 15-year
loans have higher risks

00:22:58.220 --> 00:22:59.510
than 30-year loans.

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:02.990
It's a deduction
you can make out

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:07.970
of this original long term
versus the original loan.

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:12.290
If you look at the term of
the loan and the riskiness,

00:23:12.290 --> 00:23:16.910
the blue means it lowered
the risk, whereas red

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:18.990
means it increased the risk.

00:23:18.990 --> 00:23:22.610
So 15 year had a high
degree of risk lowering

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:24.860
and a high degree
of risk increase.

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:34.700
So it had a wide range of
contribution towards the risk.

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:38.660
On the right side, you
can see California loan.

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:41.270
I have results on
many other cities.

00:23:41.270 --> 00:23:42.890
So whenever I go
make presentation,

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:46.140
I can switch to Illinois, to
New York, to other places.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:48.040
But here, we are
talking in California.

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:51.350
So you can say California
properties have a lower risk

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:55.910
because even though it makes a
negative contribution on risk,

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:58.730
which means that it
increases the risk.

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:02.540
But the increases
of a smaller amount

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:06.320
compared the range of values,
the intensity is smaller.

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:09.590
Whereas any other
state, the risk increase

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:13.880
of a much higher
intensity and the decrease

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:15.260
is of a smaller value.

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:18.320
So clearly, there
is a bigger risk--

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:21.650
any other state but California.

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:24.620
And I don't know if
that is true or not,

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:27.040
but this data shows that.

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:28.910
So that's a great example.

00:24:28.910 --> 00:24:30.170
So welcome, Adam.

00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:34.960
He's going to talk about the
second use case, insurance loss

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:35.890
prevention.

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:36.980
Please.

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:37.730
Thank you.

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:41.020
ADAM HAMMOND: Thanks, Chanchal.

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:43.725
So the use case that I wanted
to speak about today to you all

00:24:43.725 --> 00:24:46.740
was insurance loss prevention.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:50.320
So here what we had done
with a particular property

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:53.970
and casualty carrier
is they wanted

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:56.160
to take a look at
the loss likelihood

00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:58.110
of their particular policies.

00:24:58.110 --> 00:24:59.760
So there are really
three elements

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:02.410
that you can consider when
you're factoring losses.

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:04.830
So that's the frequency
that happens, so

00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:06.120
whether it will occur or not.

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:09.420
The severity of it, so how
much will that loss be.

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:10.950
And the timing of it.

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:12.510
For this particular
carrier in line

00:25:12.510 --> 00:25:15.480
of business and that carrier,
the crucial thing for them

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:17.550
was whether or not
a loss occurred,

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:19.363
because the majority
of those losses

00:25:19.363 --> 00:25:21.780
were greater than the premiums,
so they didn't necessarily

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:22.850
care about the severity.

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:26.180
Anytime you had a loss,
it would be an issue.

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:28.680
To recreate this analysis, so
the data would be a little bit

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:31.070
different here, I took
some open source data

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:33.810
that's available through
Google data set search.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:37.600
And it was Iowa Property and
Casualty losses data set.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:39.930
It also contains a
little bit of information

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:41.500
from the surrounding states.

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:43.950
So whereas Chanchal chose an
active state that we're in,

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:46.740
California, I chose one
that don't visit too often

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:49.110
for conferences in Iowa.

00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:52.740
But our solution was
ML-based, and that's

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:54.750
from policy information.

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:56.940
And then one component
that we added to that

00:25:56.940 --> 00:25:58.510
was crime score details.

00:25:58.510 --> 00:26:01.170
So based on the location
of a particular policy,

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:03.995
the crime score
could be different.

00:26:03.995 --> 00:26:05.370
And what we wanted
to do is to be

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:08.310
able to predict that policy's
likelihood of resulting

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:09.840
in a loss.

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:12.840
Some examples in terms of
why frequency is important,

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:15.600
just linked to couple
articles in the top right.

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:17.460
And then different
examples of insurance

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:20.300
that are out there where some
similar analysis can be done

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:23.760
is in property casualty,
which really focused on here.

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:26.332
But also we've seen it in
auto, professional lines,

00:26:26.332 --> 00:26:27.540
and health insurance as well.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:33.200
So first getting into the
data preparation results.

00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:37.410
So here we didn't quite have
the issues with class imbalance

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:40.460
to the same level as
Chanchal's use case.

00:26:40.460 --> 00:26:42.740
But what we saw was that
the majority of policies

00:26:42.740 --> 00:26:44.970
resulted in not having a loss.

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:48.160
And then about 40% resulted in
having a loss from that data

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:49.160
set.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:51.440
Some additions that we
made were a loss flag.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:53.690
So in the data set, if
you look this up online,

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:56.930
it will just contain the
value of a particular loss.

00:26:56.930 --> 00:26:59.630
So we wanted to create a 1, 0,
since we're really just looking

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:01.670
for the likelihood
and not the severity.

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:03.900
We added some crime
score index information.

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:06.980
So again, based on
the location, which

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.200
we randomized based on
the state that they're in,

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:12.982
we just added crime
score index data to that.

00:27:12.982 --> 00:27:14.690
The results that we
were able to generate

00:27:14.690 --> 00:27:18.170
by running just a pretty simple,
straightforward random forest

00:27:18.170 --> 00:27:22.130
here was an AUC of 0.91.

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:24.800
And the way that we compared
it was with the false positive

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:26.120
rate and true positive rate.

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:29.600
Basically, at smaller levels
of that false positive rate,

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:31.680
you want to have a higher
true positive rate.

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:35.810
Meaning those that you
forecast or predict as positive

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:37.310
are actually going
to be positive.

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:42.750
Those that we say that will have
a loss actually do have a loss.

00:27:42.750 --> 00:27:45.920
So when we look at that at
individual threshold levels,

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:48.320
if we look at the false
positive rate of 1%,

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:50.210
we can see that the
true positive rate there

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:52.010
that we have is 40%.

00:27:52.010 --> 00:27:55.020
And we expect that to
increase over time.

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:57.440
So at 10% of the
false positives,

00:27:57.440 --> 00:28:00.960
we'd have 78% of
the true positives.

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:04.130
So anything really above an
equal false positive and true

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:09.130
positive rate would indicate
a well performing model.

00:28:09.130 --> 00:28:10.810
When we go through
this process, there

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:13.540
are a couple of different ways
that we make it interpretable.

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:14.930
Some of these we've seen before.

00:28:14.930 --> 00:28:16.850
So I'll gloss over
them at a high level.

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:19.570
But the first thing we do, often
before we even start modeling,

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:22.160
is look at the
feature correlations.

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:23.580
So understand
whether or not there

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:27.220
may be a positive or negative
effect on the outcome,

00:28:27.220 --> 00:28:30.370
as well as if any
features have correlations

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:34.250
with each other as your
independent features.

00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:35.980
So what this will
do is shed light

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:38.290
on the different
relationships that we see.

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:40.850
So you can easily see
a plot of the data

00:28:40.850 --> 00:28:44.630
if I go down the diagonal axis
from top right to bottom right.

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:46.750
Then we can see the
scatter plots, which

00:28:46.750 --> 00:28:48.680
would be between two fields.

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:51.440
So if I look at these
second row, first column,

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:53.560
basically that's
showcasing my scatter plot

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:56.330
for premiums written
versus taxes paid.

00:28:56.330 --> 00:28:59.150
So we'd expect some alignment
or correlation there.

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:01.520
And then on the
right-hand side of it,

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:04.210
so if I look at the
first row, second column,

00:29:04.210 --> 00:29:06.310
I'm seeing the correlation
between those two,

00:29:06.310 --> 00:29:08.630
between premiums
written and taxes paid.

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:12.130
So this is often how we begin to
wrap our minds around the data

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:13.810
that we take a look at.

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:17.200
The second thing that we do
is once we perform the model

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:19.180
and we want to understand
a little bit more

00:29:19.180 --> 00:29:21.370
on that global
interpretability front

00:29:21.370 --> 00:29:23.553
is we look at the
feature importance.

00:29:23.553 --> 00:29:24.970
So here what we're
just looking at

00:29:24.970 --> 00:29:26.890
is the mean decrease
in Gini, and we're

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:29.560
able to see that the
important features here

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:34.330
are crime index, premiums
written, and line of insurance.

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:35.890
Now as Chanchal
pointed out earlier,

00:29:35.890 --> 00:29:39.110
a great thing to add to this
is going to be your variance.

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:42.207
So you can tell sample by
sample how this changes.

00:29:42.207 --> 00:29:44.290
We didn't really have a
high variance in this one,

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:47.440
so didn't necessarily
add them to the visual.

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:49.660
But this also needs
to be combined

00:29:49.660 --> 00:29:53.260
with some exploratory analysis
to understand the direction.

00:29:53.260 --> 00:29:55.340
So just because we know
a feature is important,

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.580
so if my crime
index is important,

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:01.862
to be able to see if I have
a loss or not, from logic

00:30:01.862 --> 00:30:03.820
here I think we can tell
which direction it is.

00:30:03.820 --> 00:30:06.040
But you'd also want some
exploratory data analysis

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:08.830
to say a higher crime
score would indicate

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:10.660
a higher likelihood of loss.

00:30:10.660 --> 00:30:12.820
So this in isolation
doesn't do well,

00:30:12.820 --> 00:30:16.360
but in combination with some
exploratory data analysis

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:20.557
can act as a way to
interpret models.

00:30:20.557 --> 00:30:22.140
The next one that
we wanted to look at

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:25.140
was again more on that local
interpretability front.

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:28.080
And the ones that we
use most frequently

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:31.050
when we're looking at problems
that are in tabular structures

00:30:31.050 --> 00:30:33.690
are going to be LIME
and going to be SHAP,

00:30:33.690 --> 00:30:37.790
as Chanchal already mentioned
what those two things are.

00:30:37.790 --> 00:30:41.340
But here rather than looking
at an overall level, one thing

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:44.160
that we've found useful
in displaying information

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:47.560
to customers is looking at
it at the observation level.

00:30:47.560 --> 00:30:50.125
So typically we may
see this in customer

00:30:50.125 --> 00:30:51.750
churn prediction
problems where they're

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:54.690
trying to identify for
a particular customer

00:30:54.690 --> 00:30:58.030
the specific reasons why
that customer might churn.

00:30:58.030 --> 00:30:59.670
So rather than
looking at all data

00:30:59.670 --> 00:31:01.770
points across all
features, we look

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:04.890
at one specific observation
across the features.

00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:07.050
So it might be for
this individual person

00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:10.500
that the written premium and
crime index are increasing

00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:12.540
their likelihood
of churn, the taxes

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:16.050
paid for it might be decreasing
their likelihood of churn,

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:19.110
versus their industry that
the property and casualty then

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:22.470
was assigned to increasing it,
and the location decreasing it.

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:24.450
But understanding that
on an individual level,

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:27.690
and then it might change from
observation to observation

00:31:27.690 --> 00:31:29.430
when I'm looking at
both LIME and SHAP

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:32.780
is extraordinarily helpful.

00:31:32.780 --> 00:31:35.330
All right, thanks, Chanchal,
now I'll hand it back to you

00:31:35.330 --> 00:31:36.530
to convert to deep Learning.

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:38.360
CHANCHAL CHATTERJEE:
Thank you, Adam.

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:41.450
Such an excellent result,
the two use cases.

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:44.540
So once again, in each
of these use cases

00:31:44.540 --> 00:31:46.340
we identified four
things that are

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:48.110
very important to regulators.

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:51.500
One is, which
inputs are important

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:54.530
and what is the intensity
and the variance of that.

00:31:54.530 --> 00:31:58.070
Secondly, how do the inputs
correlate to the outcomes,

00:31:58.070 --> 00:32:00.320
contribute to the
outcomes in a positive way

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:01.130
and a negative way.

00:32:01.130 --> 00:32:03.260
That is, which one
increases the outcome, which

00:32:03.260 --> 00:32:04.370
decreases the outcome.

00:32:04.370 --> 00:32:08.030
In Adam's case, it
was loss prediction.

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:13.880
In the example I took with
Pluto7 was in loan delinquency

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:14.610
prediction.

00:32:14.610 --> 00:32:15.530
That's the outcome.

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:20.540
So which inputs make it larger,
which inputs make it smaller.

00:32:20.540 --> 00:32:24.230
The third thing, how
do different inputs

00:32:24.230 --> 00:32:28.380
correlate with each other in
contributing to the outcome.

00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:35.510
So for example, you might find
that a certain amount of loan

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:39.650
may not matter if it is
taken from certain banks.

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:43.070
So maybe the large banks
control the loan industry.

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:45.380
We didn't find that
result, but say that we

00:32:45.380 --> 00:32:47.480
could find a result like that.

00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:51.290
It does not matter what the
amount is as long as you take

00:32:51.290 --> 00:32:53.070
it from these banks, right?

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:56.930
Something you see significant
results like that.

00:32:56.930 --> 00:32:59.510
And sometimes you
see how relationships

00:32:59.510 --> 00:33:02.780
between you and the banking
makes the difference.

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:05.700
And we sometimes find those
as important criteria.

00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:09.590
So the correlation
between different inputs,

00:33:09.590 --> 00:33:16.340
the person like the lender, and
the person who is lending to,

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:17.730
the relationship matters.

00:33:17.730 --> 00:33:22.230
So the correlations
demonstrate that.

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:26.330
And lastly is how do
these different inputs

00:33:26.330 --> 00:33:30.360
change from sample to sample
are very important things.

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:36.470
And finally, I'm going to show
a very strong and innovative

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:39.710
research that came
out of Google Research

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:43.760
by two folks, Nicholas
Frosst and Geoffrey Hinton.

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:47.810
Geoffrey Hinton is obviously
very, very, very well known.

00:33:47.810 --> 00:33:50.970
Won the Turing Award
just a few days ago.

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:52.430
Nicholas is a student.

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:57.380
And we thought that this is
a very significant result

00:33:57.380 --> 00:34:05.000
in order to do what is called an
augmenting of an existing rule

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:07.040
by creating a soft
decision tree.

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:16.590
Is my audio still working?

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:20.940
Let's say you've built a complex
model, a deep learning model.

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:24.230
But a lot of the
time, your regulators

00:34:24.230 --> 00:34:26.960
will not allow you
to use that model.

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:31.370
They will say, distill
it down to a few rules

00:34:31.370 --> 00:34:36.650
that you can add to an existing
solution that's already there

00:34:36.650 --> 00:34:38.630
which has passed regulation.

00:34:38.630 --> 00:34:42.780
We want to add more rules
to that, that's what we do.

00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:45.830
So a typical method
to solve this problem

00:34:45.830 --> 00:34:48.600
would be to take that
deep learning network--

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:50.929
here's the input,
here's the output.

00:34:50.929 --> 00:34:53.210
And take that same
input and output

00:34:53.210 --> 00:34:57.020
and create a random
forest or a decision tree.

00:34:57.020 --> 00:35:01.130
What happens is that the
results drastically drop.

00:35:01.130 --> 00:35:06.170
And let's say your complex
deep learning model did 99%.

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:10.040
All of a sudden the
decision tree does 80%.

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:13.100
So there have got to be a
solution in between where

00:35:13.100 --> 00:35:16.910
do you take that deep learning
model and convert it to rules,

00:35:16.910 --> 00:35:19.820
but you do not lose
the performance to that

00:35:19.820 --> 00:35:23.940
drastic a degree, that you come
up with something in between.

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:28.160
And this result is that, that it
produces the results somewhere

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:29.300
in between.

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:31.910
It basically takes the
deep learning model

00:35:31.910 --> 00:35:35.300
and tries to understand what
the inner workings of the models

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:37.610
are, what are the
gradients at each layer.

00:35:37.610 --> 00:35:41.570
So that you can take that
make it into a decision tree.

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:43.430
This particular
picture on the right

00:35:43.430 --> 00:35:45.950
is from a game
called Connect Four.

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:51.290
And so the main idea of
the soft decision tree

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:54.680
is to augment existing
rules, find features

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:58.590
that have significant influence
on the input, on the outcome,

00:35:58.590 --> 00:36:02.520
and create a human
interpretable rule.

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:07.760
So a binary decision tree
that has learned filters

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:11.930
at each of the
nodes is basically

00:36:11.930 --> 00:36:14.940
a multinomial
distribution function.

00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:17.720
And then when you do
a stochastic gradient

00:36:17.720 --> 00:36:19.970
on that, which is
the back propagation

00:36:19.970 --> 00:36:23.660
algorithm by which you learn
a deep learning network,

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:27.980
then you use a
equation like that

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:31.460
when you sum a multinomial
distribution over all layers

00:36:31.460 --> 00:36:34.300
of the network, and then you're
running a stochastic gradient

00:36:34.300 --> 00:36:35.990
descent.

00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:42.580
So what we do is that
we took the MNIST data.

00:36:42.580 --> 00:36:46.890
And then we learned the filters
can be understood by a person.

00:36:46.890 --> 00:36:49.810
And then errors can
be easily understood.

00:36:49.810 --> 00:36:53.200
And then the branch numbers
indicate the potential

00:36:53.200 --> 00:36:55.240
for the classification.

00:36:55.240 --> 00:36:58.770
This is classification
of digits.

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:03.670
Then the leaf number indicates
the final classification.

00:37:03.670 --> 00:37:06.100
So what they did
in their research,

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:11.050
they took this complex decision,
the convolution neural network.

00:37:11.050 --> 00:37:14.800
And they learned layer by
layer what the decision was,

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:17.830
and they converted that
to a decision tree.

00:37:17.830 --> 00:37:21.850
By that, they got
to 96% accuracy

00:37:21.850 --> 00:37:25.270
in solving it that way.

00:37:25.270 --> 00:37:29.660
So this chart demonstrates
on three examples.

00:37:29.660 --> 00:37:33.490
One is MNIST, the other is
Connect Four, and the letters.

00:37:33.490 --> 00:37:39.250
So the deep network gave
very high accuracy, like 99%.

00:37:39.250 --> 00:37:40.780
Letters, 95%.

00:37:40.780 --> 00:37:44.930
Connect Four is 87.7%.

00:37:44.930 --> 00:37:47.440
But when you fit
a decision tree,

00:37:47.440 --> 00:37:49.990
then the performance
drops drastically.

00:37:49.990 --> 00:37:53.920
You can see 87% going to 78%.

00:37:53.920 --> 00:37:58.450
So anywhere from 5% to
20%, in some instances.

00:37:58.450 --> 00:38:02.410
As you can see, a
95% drop to 78%.

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:05.080
But using this
decision tree approach

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:07.850
to the soft decision
tree approach,

00:38:07.850 --> 00:38:10.210
we got a result in between.

00:38:10.210 --> 00:38:14.860
So we created a human
readable rule, which

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:17.760
you can use to augment
your existing rules,

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:19.870
satisfy the regulations.

00:38:19.870 --> 00:38:23.380
But yet you don't have as
bad a performance as fitting

00:38:23.380 --> 00:38:26.840
a simple decision tree.

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:30.240
So that's all I have.

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:31.280
Thank you very much.

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:36.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

